      "answer": "$a=5/3\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}=1.67\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$; $\\mu=2/5=0.4$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$5=3a$ — M1; $v=u+at$ with $t=3$, $u=0$, $v=5$. $a=5/3=1.67$ — A1. $R_A=3$, $R_B=15\\cos36.9^\\circ=12$ — B1; either reaction. $F_A=3\\mu$, $F_B=12\\mu$ — M1; use $F=\\mu R$ for either term. Either Newton's-law method: for $A$, $T-F_A=0.3a$; for $B$, $15\\sin36.9^\\circ-T-F_B=1.5a$; or system equation $1.5g\\sin36.9^\\circ-F_A-F_B=1.8a$ — M1; apply Newton's second law to $A$, $B$ or the system. A2/1/0: A1 for a correct equation for $A$ or $B$; A2 for correct equations for both, or A2 for a correct system equation. $9-15\\mu=3$ — M1; solve for $\\mu$ from equations with the correct number of terms. $\\mu=0.4=2/5$ — A1. Or work-energy method: $s=\\tfrac12(5/3)\\times3^2=7.5$ — B1; distance in 3 s. PE loss $=1.5\\times10\\times7.5\\times(3/5)=67.5$ — B1. KE gain $=\\tfrac12(1.8)\\times5^2=22.5$ — B1. $67.5=22.5+3\\mu\\times7.5+12\\mu\\times7.5$ — M1; work/energy equation. $\\mu=2/5=0.4$ — A1. Total: 9.",
